{"name":"Chanterelle","hex":"#ffc66e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chanterelle","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A widely distributed edible mushroom, Cantharellus cibarius, being yellow and trumpet-shaped; or any similar mushroom of the genera Cantharellus, Polyozellus or Gomphus, not all of which are edible.","The highest string of the violin or similar instrument."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chanterelle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chanterelle","blocks":[{"text":"Chanterelle is the common name of several species of agaricomycetous fungi in the genera Cantharellus, Craterellus, Gomphus and Polyozellus. These fungi are orange, yellow or white, meaty and funnel-shaped. On the lower surface, most species have rounded, forked folds that run almost all the way down the stipe, which tapers down from the cap. Many species emit a fruity aroma and often have a mildly peppery taste."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/2007-07-14_Cantharellus_cibarius_Detail.jpg/330px-2007-07-14_Cantharellus_cibarius_Detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanterelle","wikidata":"Q3942018"},{"title":"Chanterelle (New York City restaurant)","blocks":[{"text":"Chanterelle was a fine dining restaurant that opened in New York's SoHo in 1979 before moving to Tribeca in 1989. The restaurant closed in 2009. David Waltuck was the chef and one of the owners. Another owner was his wife Karen."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanterelle_(New_York_City_restaurant)","wikidata":"Q114492641"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg/500px-Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg/1280px-Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or